---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DocsSidebar from "../components/DocsSidebar.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const deprecatedSnippet = `export interface DialogProps {
  /** @deprecated Prefer \`classNames.root\`. Still applied to the root. */
  className?: ClassNameValue<DialogState>;
  /** Typed class hooks for every structural slot. */
  classNames?: DialogClassNames;
}`;
---

<BaseLayout title="Browser support and versioning" description="Which browsers Kernel supports, which features are progressive enhancements, what counts as a breaking change, and how deprecations work." withSidebar>
  <DocsSidebar slot="sidebar" />
  <div class="docs-prose">
    <h1>Browser support and versioning</h1>
    <p>
      Kernel builds on native elements and current CSS, so the browsers it
      supports are set by the platform features it can't work without. Newer
      features are used as progressive enhancements: where a browser lacks
      one, the component still works and loses only a refinement, such as an
      animation.
    </p>

    <h2 id="browsers">Supported browsers</h2>
    <p>
      Every component works fully in these browsers and every later version:
    </p>
    <div class="docs-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Minimum browser versions"><table class="docs-table">
      <caption>Minimum browser versions.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Browser</th><th scope="col">Minimum version</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Chrome and Edge (and other Chromium browsers)</td><td>123</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Firefox</td><td>125</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Safari on macOS, iOS and iPadOS</td><td>17.5</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      Internet Explorer, legacy Edge (EdgeHTML) and any browser without the
      Popover API aren't supported. The minimums come from the features in
      the next table. The packages' compiled JavaScript and CSS target Vite's
      default build target (Chrome and Edge 111, Firefox 114, Safari 16.4),
      so the code parses in older browsers, but menus, popovers and colours
      need the versions above.
    </p>

    <h3 id="required">Features Kernel requires</h3>
    <div class="docs-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Features Kernel requires"><table class="docs-table">
      <caption>Platform features with no fallback, and the browser versions that set the minimums.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Feature</th><th scope="col">Used by</th><th scope="col">Chrome / Firefox / Safari</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Popover API (<code>popover</code>, <code>showPopover()</code>)</td><td>DropdownMenu, Popover, Tooltip, HoverCard, Combobox, ContextMenu, NavigationMenu</td><td>114 / 125 / 17</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>light-dark()</code></td><td>Every colour token in <code>@kernelui-lib/styles</code>, which ships as source CSS</td><td>123 / 120 / 17.5</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>:has()</code></td><td>State styling in Button, Badge, Toggle, RadioGroup, Slider, TagInput, Composer and others</td><td>105 / 121 / 15.4</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>oklch()</code> and <code>color-mix()</code></td><td>The colour scales and tints</td><td>111 / 113 / 16.2</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> and <code>showModal()</code></td><td>Dialog, Sheet, CommandPalette</td><td>All supported versions</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Custom Elements v1</td><td><code>@kernelui-lib/elements</code></td><td>All supported versions</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      The component stylesheets are compiled with Lightning CSS, which turns
      <code>light-dark()</code> into a fallback that older browsers understand.
      The tokens in <code>@kernelui-lib/styles</code> ship uncompiled. If
      your bundler lowers CSS for older targets, as Vite 8 does in production
      builds, the tokens get the same fallback.
    </p>

    <h3 id="progressive-enhancement">Progressive enhancements</h3>
    <p>
      These features are used only where the browser supports them. Each row
      says what happens in a browser that doesn't.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Progressive enhancements"><table class="docs-table">
      <caption>Features Kernel uses when available, and the fallback in other browsers.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Feature</th><th scope="col">Used for</th><th scope="col">Without it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>CSS anchor positioning</td><td>Placing menus, popovers, tooltips and the combobox list next to their trigger</td><td>Positioned by measuring the trigger in JavaScript and kept inside the viewport, but not flipped to the opposite side when space runs out</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>overlay</code> with <code>transition-behavior: allow-discrete</code></td><td>Exit animations for <code>popover="auto"</code> panels (Chromium only)</td><td>The panel closes without an exit animation. A guard in the reset turns the exit off rather than let the panel jump while it fades</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>@starting-style</code></td><td>Entry animations for dialogs, sheets, menus and popovers</td><td>Surfaces appear without animating</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>::details-content</code> and <code>interpolate-size</code></td><td>Animating Accordion and other disclosures open and closed</td><td>They open and close instantly</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Exclusive <code>&lt;details name&gt;</code></td><td>Accordion items that close each other (Firefox 130)</td><td>In Firefox 125 to 129, several items can be open at once</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Container style queries</td><td>Icon stroke width set with <code>--kernel-icon-stroke-width</code> (Safari 18; not yet in Firefox)</td><td>Icons keep their default stroke</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>field-sizing: content</code></td><td>Composer and Textarea growing with their text</td><td>A fallback in each component sizes the field</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>appearance: base-select</code> and <code>::picker(select)</code></td><td>A Kernel-styled Select dropdown</td><td>The browser's own picker opens; the closed Select is still styled</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>closedby="any"</code></td><td>Light dismiss for Dialog</td><td>A click-outside handler closes the dialog, and Escape works everywhere</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>contrast-color()</code></td><td>Choosing black or white text on any accent hue</td><td>Each accent preset's own text colour is used</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scroll-driven animations</td><td>Scroll-edge shadows on ScrollArea and Table</td><td>ScrollArea updates its shadows from JavaScript; Table shows no edge fade</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>cap</code> and <code>lh</code> units, typed <code>calc()</code></td><td>Optical centring of text and icons in pills (Badge, Button, Toggle and others)</td><td>Each rule's <code>@supports</code> fallback uses plain padding, so text can sit slightly low</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>text-wrap: balance</code> and <code>pretty</code></td><td>Headings and paragraphs</td><td>Text wraps normally</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      Motion follows <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> in every browser:
      each component that moves swaps travel and scale for a short fade.
      Right-to-left layouts use logical properties, and the few rules that
      need <code>:dir(rtl)</code> also match <code>[dir="rtl"]</code>,
      because the build rewrites <code>:dir()</code> into a list of
      languages.
    </p>

    <h2 id="runtimes">Node.js and React</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong><code>@kernelui-lib/react</code></strong> needs React and
        ReactDOM 18 or newer (peer dependencies <code>&gt;=18.0.0</code>).
        React 19 is supported, and the docs site runs on it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>@kernelui-lib/elements</code></strong> and
        <strong><code>@kernelui-lib/styles</code></strong> have no runtime
        dependencies beyond the browser.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>@kernelui-lib/cli</code></strong>, including the MCP
        server, needs Node.js 20 or newer.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="versioning">Versioning</h2>
    <p>
      Every package follows <a href="https://semver.org/">semantic versioning</a>
      and is versioned on its own with Changesets. Each release's changelog
      is on GitHub and in the package. Version 1.x gets fixes and security
      updates; 0.x doesn't.
    </p>

    <h3 id="public-api">What the version covers</h3>
    <p>These are Kernel's public API. Breaking any of them needs a major release:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>React:</strong> exported components, hooks, utilities and types; prop names, types, accepted values and defaults; callback signatures; and <code>labels</code> keys.</li>
      <li><strong>Custom Elements:</strong> tag names, attributes (including <code>label-*</code>), properties, events and their <code>detail</code> shape, and slots.</li>
      <li><strong>Rendered HTML:</strong> the native element each component renders (Button is a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>, Dialog a <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>), and documented <code>data-slot</code> and <code>data-state</code> attributes.</li>
      <li><strong>CSS:</strong> component class names (<code>kernel-Button-root</code> and the like, kept stable so you can target them), <code>--kernel-*</code> custom property names, the stylesheet entry points (<code>styles.css</code>, <code>styles.layer.css</code> and the <code>@kernelui-lib/styles</code> subpaths) and the cascade layer names.</li>
      <li><strong>CLI:</strong> commands, flags, exit codes, and the MCP server's tool names and inputs.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="breaking">Major, minor and patch</h3>
    <div class="docs-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Release types"><table class="docs-table">
      <caption>Which release each kind of change goes in.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Release</th><th scope="col">Changes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Major</td><td>Removing or renaming anything in the public API; accepting fewer values; changing a default in a way that changes behaviour; rendering a different native element; changing an event's <code>detail</code>; raising the minimum React, Node.js or browser version</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Minor</td><td>New components, props, attributes, variants and tokens; deprecations; design refinements that keep the API, including new token values</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Patch</td><td>Bug fixes and accessibility fixes, including ones that add ARIA attributes or adjust markup inside a component</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      Token values (the exact colours, spacing and radii) and internal markup
      that isn't listed above aren't part of the API, so they can change in a
      minor or patch release. If you snapshot Kernel's rendered output in
      tests, expect those snapshots to change between minor releases.
    </p>

    <h3 id="deprecation">Deprecations</h3>
    <p>Nothing is removed without notice. A deprecated API goes through these steps:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        A minor release marks it with a JSDoc <code>@deprecated</code> tag that
        names the replacement, so your editor strikes it through, and lists it
        in the changelog. Custom Element attributes are marked in the docs and
        <code>llms.txt</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        From that release, using it logs one console warning per page load in
        development, naming the replacement. Production builds stay silent.
      </li>
      <li>
        It keeps working for at least one more minor release.
      </li>
      <li>
        The next major release removes it, and its changelog says how to
        migrate.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      An example from Dialog. Passing <code>className</code> logs a one-time
      warning in development that points to <code>classNames.root</code>:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={deprecatedSnippet} lang="tsx" />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
